{
  "_domain": "Whether an audio state names a track, as three readers decide it: Milō's screen (`richSourceFor`, frontend/src/composables/useRichDisplay.js — a full view rather than the source card), its Lock Screen push (`payloads.shown_track`, backend/core/push/payloads.py — a track rather than the source card) and Milo-iOS / Milo-Mac (`MiloAudioState.shown`). Only the first two are run against these rows: no Swift runs in Milō's suite, so the apps' rule is pinned word for word by test_milo_ios_contract.py::test_the_apps_name_a_connected_session_as_milo_does and run by their own MiloAudioStateTests. The rule they share: a CONNECTED session — a sender Milō cannot call playing or paused — names its track only with a title, an artist and a cover (D14); any other session names it with a title. Read by backend/tests/contracts/test_naming_rule.py and frontend/tests/architecture/namingRule.test.js. Covered: sources without a browser (AirPlay, Bluetooth, Mac), with `service` running, `switching` false and no availability reason, and AirPlay covers wider than 300 px. Left out on purpose, because the readers differ there by design: the 300 px gate on an AirPlay sender's cover (the kiosk's own image-quality call, never the Lock Screen's), the browser sources (their view is drawn whatever the session names), and an availability reason or a source change (both draw the card on Milō's screen whatever the push shows). Every state is a whole wire state (`AudioState`), validated as such.",
